Hi Sam and Brandon,

Hi Sam,

I checked whether we can assign RMM monitors for ADSync and DFS Replication. We can monitor both through Windows Service monitors and Event Log monitors.

We already have a Windows Service monitor configured for the ADSync service. However, monitoring only the service state does not confirm that ADSync is actually functioning correctly. To verify the actual synchronization status, I believe we will need a custom PowerShell-based monitor. I will discuss this further with Mark.

For DFS Replication, there are specific Event IDs that we can monitor. We can enable the required event-log monitoring across the DC servers through Group Policy and use this to detect DFS replication issues.

One consideration is that we run hourly backups, during which DFS Replication may temporarily pause. Therefore, we need to make sure the monitoring logic accounts for these expected pauses; otherwise, we could generate unnecessary alerts.


Hi Brandon,

During the 4th-week patch cycle, ESWGM-AZ-DC01 did not receive any patches. The last patches installed on this server were on 12 August.

Around the time of the 4th-week patching, I also noticed that the server's Network Connection Profile was showing as DomainAuthenticated. I suspect that some previously configured changes or settings may have been affected by, or become active following, the reboot performed during the 4th-week maintenance window. This is currently a suspicion and requires further investigation.

I followed the CCR and performed the reboots as instructed. However, the AD replication issue remained after the reboot.

To further analyse the incident, I also followed several Microsoft troubleshooting procedures and performed additional verification checks. I did not make any configuration changes during these checks; I only ran diagnostic and verification commands.

The following are the key findings:
ESWGM-AZ-DC01 shares cannot be accessed from the other domain controllers.
Attempts to access shares on ESWGM-AZ-DC01 from the other DCs resulted in connectivity/name-resolution-related errors.

The domain secure-channel verification from ESWGM-AZ-DC01 is failing.
nltest /sc_query and nltest /sc_verify on ESWGM-AZ-DC01 returned errors indicating that the domain could not be contacted/verified. netdom verify also failed with a similar result.

Basic network connectivity and required AD communication ports are available.
I tested connectivity between the domain controllers using Test-NetConnection for the key Active Directory communication ports, including RPC (TCP 135), SMB (TCP 445), LDAP (TCP 389), LDAPS (TCP 636), Kerberos (TCP 88), and DNS (TCP 53). The connectivity tests were successful. Therefore, there does not appear to be a basic network, firewall, RPC, SMB, LDAP, Kerberos, or DNS port-connectivity issue between the domain controllers.

There is an indication of a computer-account password synchronization issue.
I used the repadmin /showobjmeta command to review and compare the Active Directory replication metadata for the ESWGM-AZ-DC01 computer account across the domain controllers. The copy of the computer account on ESWGM-AZ-DC01 shows a newer pwdLastSet/password-related metadata version (26 August, Version 11), while the copies held by ESWGM-DC01 and ESWGM-LA-DC01 still show the previous password state (27 July, Version 10).

This indicates that the ESWGM-AZ-DC01 computer-account password information may not be synchronized across the domain controllers, which could contribute to the Kerberos authentication failure and the 0x80090322 – The target principal name is incorrect replication error.

After completing the reboot, I followed the CCR's validation/test step by signing in to the W365 PC and confirming that the shared I: drive was accessible and that STSAdmin Pro loaded successfully. These user-facing services are currently functioning, although the underlying AD replication issue remains unresolved.
